linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3 & -1 \\\\ -10 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & -3-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -10 & 0 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )((8-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-2(-10)) & =0 \\\\ (-3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +52) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=-3.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{12\\pm \\sqrt{(-12)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 52}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2fc383c6c9a7184f366816aa22799e9ae44a9bbcadd70d0919144630611ce4d9"} {"question" "q-135533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135533" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "B=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 4-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -5 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )^{2}-5(-5)) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +29) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=4.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 29}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-100}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 10\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2fc383c6c9a7184f366816aa22799e9ae44a9bbcadd70d0919144630611ce4d9"} {"question" "q-135534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135534" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2\\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PB{P}^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )^{2}-4(-9)) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +40) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n${\\lambda }_{1}=5.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{(-4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 40}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm \\sqrt{-144}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pm 12\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\pm 6\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135530" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2fc383c6c9a7184f366816aa22799e9ae44a9bbcadd70d0919144630611ce4d9"} {"question" "q-135530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "B=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135530" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3+2\\text{i} \\\\ 4+\\text{i} \\\\ 2+6\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4-\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{3},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & {v}_{3} \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{3}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135641" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2fc383c6c9a7184f366816aa22799e9ae44a9bbcadd70d0919144630611ce4d9"} {"question" "q-135641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 6 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135641" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2\\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2+5\\text{i} \\\\ 1+3\\text{i} \\\\ 4-7\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2+7\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{3},$ then $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{2}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{2})\\end{aligned}],\\,\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & \\text{Re}({v}_{2}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{2}) \\\\ | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & -7\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135667" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2fc383c6c9a7184f366816aa22799e9ae44a9bbcadd70d0919144630611ce4d9"} {"question" "q-135667" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/ty