et{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 4 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135541" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2fc383c6c9a7184f366816aa22799e9ae44a9bbcadd70d0919144630611ce4d9"} {"question" "q-135541" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/r