=[\\begin{aligned}-2-6\\text{i} \\\\ -3+2\\text{i} \\\\ -6+\\text{i} \\\\ -5-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3-7\\text{i}$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2-4\\text{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{3}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{4},$ \n\nthen $A=PB{P}^{-1},$ where\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{1}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\text{Im}({\\lambda }_{3}) & \\text{Re}({\\lambda }_{3})\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ \\text{Re}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{1}) & \\text{Re}({v}_{3}) & \\text{Im}({v}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 & 2\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{1},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ correspond to the complex eigenvector ${v}_{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -6 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135536" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2fc383c6c9a7184f366816aa22799e9ae44a9bbcadd70d0919144630611ce4d9"} {"question" "q-135536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -5 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -6 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -5 & -1 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -6 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -6 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 & -3 \\\\ -7 & 4 & 1 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 4 & -6 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "q-135536" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -7 & 4 & -6 & 1 \\\\ -6 & 4 & -5 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/state.json" "file_sha256" "ce805066c1aa047de8640e8e15e03d4ab24fb938955a97cd746937fef1b5f487"} {"question" "e-5906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/139905ee461ed3a946aa232e42e65f619f1550884b24dc72354303e3af7a05df.png)\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/example-5906.json" "file_sha256" "00b56c29db3bebacb4eb0db6eb5b90ff6c3bc53b572a6e65313e4c451773d59e"} {"question" "e-5906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/66747ae8628c72623aa51610ed39af91154d06d61e67189f93d04079b64f494a.png)\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/eef90c4d866110a74a221ce82ad275de5b07ddbf6e33213d94cf5f7831295022.png)\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_{1,2}=1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\color{red}\\lambda_3=-3}.$\n\n- From our matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/5449e99fabebae1514f40bdf40698e84bb60a578e495e5a363ed7ac617749ad2.png)\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/cdf7fb1cb310f63e0dcf468aa045dc0fb1bb3b2e757ae431ae499c898ab5538f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/example-5906.json" "file_sha256" "00b56c29db3bebacb4eb0db6eb5b90ff6c3bc53b572a6e65313e4c451773d59e"} {"question" "e-5533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/7014ce766be779f4231c78a99a6e013bfc48bfd9e21da7fa565c49e466437c99.png)\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B,$ where the $2 \\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PBP^{-1}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/example-5533.json" "file_sha256" "ed69b918a937ae2ad8e35840bc2c9195b7474c333d2031cc9ca5e61c5eb55d34"} {"question" "e-5533" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/5e03c481312af462a0739b4670e24325d2e06cc10449c65bfb04208206fc5d58.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2-\\lambda  & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((-2-\\lambda )^{2}-1(-1)) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is \n$\\lambda_1={\\color{red}-1}.$\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{2,3} & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{(4)^{2}-4\\cdot 1\\cdot 5}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4\\pm 2\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/712c3f663a367090ab5ba81705e7c7f36ce6d4e2d6e2b13cc59383421e408ea7.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/example-5533.json" "file_sha256" "ed69b918a937ae2ad8e35840bc2c9195b7474c333d2031cc9ca5e61c5eb55d34"} {"question" "e-5534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/31a6051588cdb215a025179138803257d01f72cb2f231cda05cbbd67b56b0fb9.png)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=1-\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find the matrix $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/example-5534.json" "file_sha256" "a10296720c26a121a61743769106288e4484aa9e08471a0b26c9e7e039d913f9"} {"question" "e-5534" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and two complex eigenvalues ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3},$ then $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/2a08ff9b84de36c2b9141809f385f688f6b8c83b2b916bd3469432705636a154.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/50b840579f0396b87913b773975c26b337c9846c139bc4a1bf9445984681f062.png)\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}},$ and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} .$ Therefore, \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/55b4ecb56daf7f8cce19878af9f17e237a5f38b1593a4000d39487cb93ff2c95.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/example-5534.json" "file_sha256" "a10296720c26a121a61743769106288e4484aa9e08471a0b26c9e7e039d913f9"} {"question" "e-5535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2 \\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/5fd08491c00587e0efa4017850407c58af8ad0821c2c1aab512ec20b5295d128.png)\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=7 + 4\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_3=-3 + 2\\textrm{i},$ respectively. \nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1},$ find a possible matrix $P.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/example-5535.json" "file_sha256" "e6fd45c5533eca97234623ea79cd2c81f69f4cb382d08f3a2eb4ac9a2d4a299e"} {"question" "e-5535" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues \n\n$\\qquad$ ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}=\\overline{\\color{red}\\lambda}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad$ and $\\quad {\\color{blue}\\lambda_3}=\\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}4}$ \n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors \n\n$\\qquad$ ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} = \\overline{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad$ and $\\quad {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}4},$ \n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1},$ where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/c8b3063b226f25c53f064e3f427446da7cc4c7cd99a20b8c1d9e46d33bd429e0.png)\nand\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/0489fb7ddf3f73682e4e61345fa73a601c5f8ae01bf319918becd9c2426cc73d.png)\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P.$\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/350a5319ff61e3f794e780561616c7a9523f18832ee040ce8faa0825818f8f24.png)\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}},$ and the third and forth columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}.$ Therefore,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/assets/c6213ccfec2acbc449e38e431ef1d60d2e06b19066b735faa6426dfd4b326c9c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/example-5535.json" "file_sha256" "e6fd45c5533eca97234623ea79cd2c81f69f4cb382d08f3a2eb4ac9a2d4a299e"} {"question" "q-38665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\mp 2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\mp \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "b04ba7ce3a533f616773eb01b768648591f7b525387dd6c3cf97dd37579aadbe" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Source-backed derived judgment: let p2=(-2,0,1) and p3=(0,-2,-1), the second and third columns of P. The rotation-scaling block gives B(0,1,i)=(-2+5i)(0,1,i), so A(p2+i p3)=(-2+5i)(p2+i p3), with eigenvector (-2,-2i,1-i). Conjugation gives eigenvalue -2-5i with eigenvector (-2,2i,1+i). Thus the paired notation is λ=-2±5i and v=(-2,∓2i,1∓i), exactly as original option b and the worked-solution screenshots show. Original index 1 is also supported by saved Correct grading. Capture serialization lost both ∓ symbols; restore them while preserving the text field type." "bindings" {"problem" "5051f90e00e71f1035042c5267cb6703a249d7c31477a4087bb085095581e6c5" "worked_solution" "38c455c25b07123a80bf7494184e6008e42a5ac9f3045bac366cf97a1d959ff0" "original_field" "701e674fb1dba977ec52622bc9c238cc7b8d982cc6bb4a2880e1d0f8f93418f4" "reviewed_field" "f5357e98eb02df5cddd36de235a769db9f2eb0de270be07027a54c65f26caf98" "raw_widget" "b24a51d75425a0d5c8930acc309d770838a8afdac05a5bd5e3da92f5e4fb740c"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-38665-before.png" "sha256" "0fd08925b00db73a1da18fb141ac88eea4db6cc17e5a07edb6f13ac745e406f8"} {"path" "q-38665-before.json" "sha256" "98563e65e322c4c232839c3d5636bb8cb6d6f7b250c271b3fd405f8bb2d86685"} {"path" "history-q-38665.png" "sha256" "dbb392d0da2533d8542cacb7c7b4bd5628b540efd69c0f3a033b5f5d399715e8"} {"path" "q-38665-after.json" "sha256" "6a276bdc83e09ba8dd22559d37832af9e42f333f173e33ac0723bd6cb8f70332"}]} {"question" "q-38665" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\pm 2\\text{i} \\\\ ∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =-2\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\mp 2\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\mp \\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =1\\pm 5\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =5\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ \\pm 2\\text{ } \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$\\lambda =5\\pm 2\\text{i}$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{i} \\\\ ∓2\\text{i} \\\\ 1∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14071697/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "b04ba7ce3a533f616773eb01b768648591f7b525387dd6c3cf97dd37579aadbe" "rationale" "Source-backed derived judgment: let p2=(-2,0,1) and p3=(0,-2,-1), the second and third columns of P. The rotation-scaling block gives B(0,1,i)=(-2+5i)(0,1,i), so A(p2+i p3)=(-2+5i)(p2+i p3), with eigenvector (-2,-2i,1-i). Conjugation gives eigenvalue -2-5i with eigenvector (-2,2i,1+i). Thus the paired notation is λ=-2±5i and v=(-2,∓2i,1∓i), exactly as original option b and the worked-solution screenshots show. Original index 1 is also supported by saved Correct grading. Capture serialization lost both ∓ symbols; restore them while preserving the text field type."}] "usage" {"e-5906" [] "e-5533" [] "q-38665" [] "e-5534" [] "q-38660" [] "e-5535" []} "basis" 2578 "retractions" [[17592186129627 :question/problem "$P = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"] [17592186129627 :question/answer-fields 17592186127556] [17592186129624 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\cdot 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$v_{1} = \\begin{bmatrix}5 + 7\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ \\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}, v_{3} = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $v_{1}$ and $v_{3}$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $λ_{1} =-1 - 2\\textrm{i}$ and $λ_{3} =-2$, respectively.\nGiven that $A = PBP^{-1}$, which of the following could be the matrix $P$?"] [17592186128913 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the nonsingular matrix $P$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown above, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1}$, what are the complex (not real) eigenvalues and the corresponding eigenvectors of $A$?"] [17592186128913 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ real matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}$, and two complex eigenvalues\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & Re(λ_{2}) & Im(λ_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -Im(λ_{2}) & Re(λ_{2})\\end{bmatrix}$\nNotice that swapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P$.\n\n- From our block diagonal matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe deduce the complex eigenvalues are ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_{1,2}=1 \\pm 3\\textrm{i}}$ and the real eigenvalue is ${\\color{red}\\lambda_3=-3}$.\n- From our matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe deduce that the complex and real eigenvectors are\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4∓\\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 ± \\textrm{i} \\\\ 1 ± 2\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186128885 :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $A$ shown above. Find a block diagonal matrix $B$, where the $2 \\times 2$ block represents a rotation-scaling matrix, such that $A=PBP^{-1}$."] [17592186128885 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}$, and two complex eigenvalues\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & Re(λ_{2}) & Im(λ_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -Im(λ_{2}) & Re(λ_{2})\\end{bmatrix}$\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P$.\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A$, we solve the characteristic equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2 - λ & 0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 - λ & 5 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -2 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(-1 - λ)((-2 - λ)^{2} - 1(-1)) &= 0 \\\\\n(-1 - λ)(λ^{2} + 4λ + 5) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the real eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1={\\color{red}-1}\n$$\n\nNow, using the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ_{2, 3} &= (-4 ± \\sqrt{(4)^{2} - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 5})/(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4 ± \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{-4 ± 2\\textrm{i}}{2} \\\\\n&=-2 ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the block diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186128886 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where the $2 \\times 2$ block of $B$ represents a rotation-scaling matrix:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2$ and $\\lambda_2=1-\\textrm{i}$, respectively.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1}$, find the matrix $P$."] [17592186128886 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a real $3\\times 3$ matrix that has one real eigenvalue ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ with corresponding eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}$, and two complex eigenvalues\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}3}\n$$\n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & Re(λ_{2}) & Im(λ_{2}) \\\\ 0 & -Im(λ_{2}) & Re(λ_{2})\\end{bmatrix}$\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P$.\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe deduce that the first column of $P$ will correspond to the real eigenvector ${{\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}}$, and the second and third columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186128887 :question/problem "Consider the vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ and the block diagonal matrix $B$ shown below, where both $2 \\times 2$ blocks of $B$ represent rotation-scaling matrices:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 + 2\\textrm{i} \\\\ -1 + 3\\textrm{i} \\\\ 4 + \\textrm{i} \\\\ -\\textrm{i}\\end{bmatrix}$\nThe vectors $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_3$ are the eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=7 + 4\\textrm{i}$ and $\\lambda_3=-3 + 2\\textrm{i}$, respectively.\nGiven that $A=PBP^{-1}$, find a possible matrix $P$."] [17592186128887 :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real matrix that has complex eigenvalues\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\lambda_1}=\\overline{\\color{red}\\lambda}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad {\\color{blue}\\lambda_3}=\\overline{\\color{blue}\\lambda}_{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nwith corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$\\qquad$\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} = \\overline{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{red}2} \\quad\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\quad {\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3} = \\overline{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}}_{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\nthen $A=PBP^{-1}$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}Re(λ_{1}) & Im(λ_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ -Im(λ_{1}) & Re(λ_{1}) & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & Re(λ_{3}) & Im(λ_{3}) \\\\ 0 & 0 & -Im(λ_{3}) & Re(λ_{3})\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$[\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; Re(v_{1})\\begin{vmatrix}Im(v_{1}) & Re(v_{3})\\end{vmatrix}Im(v_{3}); \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid]$\nSwapping the blocks on the main diagonal of $B$ will result in switching the corresponding columns in $P$.\n\nFrom our block diagonal matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$\nwe deduce that the first and second columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{red}{\\mathbf{v}_1}}$, and the third and forth columns of $P$ will correspond to the complex eigenvector ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_3}$. Therefore,\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186127539 17592186127540 17592186127541 17592186127542 17592186127543 17592186127557 17592186127558 17592186127559 17592186127560 17592186127561] "immutable_fields" [[17592186127538 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127538 :answer-field/id] [17592186127538 :answer-field/key] [17592186127538 :answer-field/type] [17592186127556 :answer-field/correct] [17592186127556 :answer-field/id] [17592186127556 :answer-field/key] [17592186127556 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
